# {{ title }}

YAMD is built on GitHub Flavored Markdown, so most Markdown works unchanged; see the [GitHub-style README](github-readme.yamd). This page shows every case where the same text renders differently, and how to get the Markdown result.

## The fast way

Run **YAMD: Convert from Markdown** on a file, or right-click a `.md` file and choose **Open Markdown as YAMD**. Both make every fix below for you.

## Line breaks

In Markdown, a single newline is a space. In YAMD, it's a line break:

Roses are red
Violets are blue

To get one paragraph, put it on one line. Paragraphs that look hard-wrapped get an info note (Y210) with a quick fix that joins them.

## YAMD-only syntax

Each of these is plain text on GitHub but means something in YAMD. A backslash keeps the Markdown result.

| You write   | YAMD shows | Escaped        | Shows                     |
| ----------- | ---------- | -------------- | ------------------------- |
| `#ideas`    | #ideas     | `\#ideas`      | \#ideas                   |
| `==key==`   | ==key==    | `\==key==`     | \==key==                  |
| `++new++`   | ++new++    | `\++new++`     | \++new++                  |
| `x^2`       | x^2        | `x\^2`         | x\^2                      |
| `H_{2}O`    | H_{2}O     | `H\_{2}O`      | H\_{2}O                   |
| `[[differences]]` | [[differences]] | `\[[differences]]` | \[[differences]]   |
| `%% hi %%`  | %% hi %%   | `\%% hi %%`    | \%% hi %%                 |

## Front matter

A `---` on the first line starts front matter in YAMD. To begin a file with a thematic break, put a blank line before it.

## Changing bullet markers

As in Markdown, changing the marker starts a new list:

- one list

* another list

## Raw HTML

HTML is sanitized with GitHub's rules, so <kbd>kbd</kbd>, <sub>sub</sub> and `<details>` work, and scripts are removed.
